e it more semantic.\n\n**Rule 4 - Fewest Elements**: \n- The structure is fairly minimal. The three steps (base, surcharge, total) are clear.\n\nLet me think about what the most valuable refactoring is:\n\n1. **Extract the magic number `0.1` as a named constant** `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE`. This improves Rule 2 (reveals intent) - the number 0.1 alone is meaningless, but `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1` reveals what it is.\n\n2. **Update the misleading comment**. It says \"Hardcoded: 1 sword\" which is wrong now.\n\nLet me also check if there are any other issues:\n\nLooking at the full file, I notice:\n- The comment \"Per-type base premium for the standard (non-component) item types.\" is good\n- The constants are well-named\n- The code structure is clear\n\nThe main refactoring opportunity I see is:\n1. Extract `0.1` magic number to a named constant `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE`\n2. Remove or update the misleading comment about \"Hardcoded: 1 sword\"\n3. Consider whether to inline `firstInsurance` since it's used only once (Rule 4 - fewest elements)\n\nLet me weigh these:\n\nOption A: Extract magic number to constant\n```typescript\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1;\n// ...\nconst firstInsurance = base * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\nreturn Math.ceil(base + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE);\n```\n- Mass: constants +1, invocations same. Net: +1 to mass\n- BUT improves Rule 2 (reveals intent)\n\nOption B: Remove `firstInsurance` variable\n```typescript\nreturn Math.ceil(base + base * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE + PROCESSING_FEE);\n```\n- Reduces bindings by 1 (mass -1), reduces assignments by 1 (mass -6), but the expression is less clear\n\nOption C: Both - extract constant AND inline firstInsurance\n```typescript\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1;\nreturn Math.ceil(base * (1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE) + PROCESSING_FEE);\n```\n- This makes the math even clearer: \"base times (1 + 10%) plus fee\"\n- Mass: constants +1, bindings -1, assignments -1, invocations -1 (no more `base + firstInsurance`)\n- Net: +1 - 1 - 6 - 2 = -8\n\nActually let me recount Option C:\n- Constants: 1 (FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE) + 1 (PROCESSING_FEE) = 2 (same as before, just shifted)\n- Bindings: items, _customer, _quoteIndex, base = 4 (was 5, removed firstInsurance)\n- Invocations: computeBasePremium, *, +, Math.ceil = 4 (was 5)\n- Assignments: 1 (was 2)\n- Total: 2 + 4 + 4 + 1*6 = 16\n\nHmm, actually let me be careful. Looking at the formula again: `base * (1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE) + PROCESSING_FEE`:\n- invocations: computeBasePremium, *, +, Math.ceil = 4 invocations (just computeBasePremium, *, +, Math.ceil)\n- Actually wait, the Math.ceil call wraps everything, so there's one + inside Math.ceil, plus the *.\n- computeBasePremium: 1\n- base * (1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE): * invocation (1)\n- 1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE: + invocation (1)\n- result + PROCESSING_FEE: + invocation (1)\n- Math.ceil(...): 1\n- Total invocations: 5\n\nSo Option C:\n- Constants: 2\n- Bindings: 4\n- Invocations: 5\n- Assignments: 1\n- Total: 2 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 17\n\nOriginal:\n- Constants: 2\n- Bindings: 5\n- Invocations: 5\n- Assignments: 2\n- Total: 2 + 5 + 5 + 12 = 24\n\nSo Option C: 24 -> 17 = 7 reduction.\n\nBut wait - Option C combines base + firstInsurance into base * (1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE). This is mathematically equivalent but actually obscures the intent slightly. The original says \"base + first insurance + fee\" which is the most natural way to express the premium formula.\n\nLet me reconsider. The formula in the spec is \"premium = base * 1.1 + 5\" (per the user's note about the recent Green phase). So `base * 1.1 + 5` is the canonical form.\n\nThe cleanest refactoring might be:\n\n```typescript\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1;\n// ...\nconst base = computeBasePremium(items);\nreturn Math.ceil(base * (1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE) + PROCESSING_FEE);\n```\n\nOr perhaps even more readable:\n\n```typescript\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst base = computeBasePremium(items);\nconst withFirstInsurance =