nal produces 115 because the floating point errors happen to cancel or round in a way that gives exactly 115.\n\nBut my refactored version: `base * (1 + 0.1)` does:\n- 1 + 0.1 = 1.1 (stored as approximately 1.1000000000000001)\n- 100 * 1.1000000000000001 = 110.00000000000001\n- + 5 = 115.00000000000001\n- Math.ceil = 116!\n\nThis is a classic floating-point refactoring trap! The order of operations matters because of floating-point representation.\n\nLet me revert to use the original structure but with the named constant:","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"A floating-point precision issue! Let me analyze:\n\n- Original: `Math.ceil(100 + (100 * 0.1) + 5)` → `100 * 0.1` = 10 (stored precisely enough), so `100 + 10 + 5 = 115` exactly → `Math.ceil(115) = 115` ✓\n- Refactored: `Math.ceil(100 * (1 + 0.1) + 5)` → `1 + 0.1 = 1.1000000000000001` (FP imprecision), so `100 * 1.1000000000000001 = 110.00000000000001`, then `+ 5 = 115.00000000000001` → `Math.ceil(...) = 116` ✗\n\nClassic FP ordering trap. Let me fix by restoring the original two-term structure but keep the named constant:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_485d5206be26456b8e149510","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"export const computePremium = (\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): number => {\n  const base = computeBasePremium(items);\n  const firstInsurance = base * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  return Math.ceil(base + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};","oldText":"export const computePremium = (\n  items: Item[],\n  _customer: Customer,\n  _quoteIndex: number,\n): number => {\n  const base = computeBasePremium(items);\n  return Math.ceil(base * (1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE) + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};"}],"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_18-58-53_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3/src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input